॰ whether a row is the HEADER — Rust's `!l.starts_with("code\t")`
॰ (nidana.rs:226). Field ० of the header is the word `code` , and there is
॰ no other way to tell it from a row: it is not a comment, it is not
॰ blank, and it carries the same four fields every other line does.
॰
॰ WRITTEN AS FOUR NUMBERS, ९९ १११ १०० १०१ , which is `code` in ASCII.
॰ R-15-1 admits no Latin literal, and ADR-0017 is explicit that a string
॰ is not a licence to smuggle a refused character back in — so this is the
॰ same move the header of this file makes for the brace and the digit
॰ zero, and the same one अक्षरकोश ॱ इत्संज्ञा makes for `it` .
